{
  "analysis": "The new lookup unpacks KeyTransform.preprocess_lhs() into two values although it returns three, so ordinary key-transform IN lookups fail before backend handling. The SQLite branch also returns a complete IN expression where only RHS SQL belongs, uses incompatible placeholders, and wraps values in JSON objects. MySQL and Oracle receive no corrective adaptation, and existing Oracle exact-null handling is removed.",
  "scores": {"R1": 1, "R2": 1, "R3": 1, "R4": 2, "R5": 3, "R6": 1, "R7": 1},
  "reasons": {
    "R1": "The implementation does not fix the reported lookup on any of the three target backends.",
    "R2": "Incorrect tuple unpacking breaks normal lookups, SQLite SQL construction is invalid, and removing KeyTransformExact.as_oracle() regresses exact-null comparisons.",
    "R3": "Numerous standalone debugging scripts and removal of unrelated exact-lookup behavior expand the change beyond the required fix.",
    "R4": "The implementation violates lookup SQL-generation contracts and Django placeholder conventions while adding redundant imports and style inconsistencies.",
    "R5": "Test changes are withheld, so regression coverage cannot be assessed and is scored neutrally.",
    "R6": "Leftover debugging scripts, redundant branches, unused imports, and misleading explanatory comments obscure the intended implementation.",
    "R7": "This requires a rewritten backend-aware RHS implementation, restoration of Oracle exact-null handling, and removal of debugging artifacts before merging."
  }
}
